% ============================================================================
% foot-bones — the three groups of bones in the human foot and the region /
% part of the foot each group occupies (adj-facts-stdlib, ANATOMY).
% ============================================================================
% The whole skeleton of the foot is built out of just THREE groups of bones,
% and each group sits in a characteristic part of the foot. Reading from the
% heel to the tips of the toes: the TARSALS are the cluster at the back that
% forms the heel and the ankle; the METATARSALS are the long bones of the
% midfoot (the body of the foot, the sole and instep); and the PHALANGES are
% the little bones that make up the toes. Before an anatomy or MEDICINE agent
% can reason about a fracture, a joint, or an X-ray of the foot it must first
% know WHICH of the three groups a bone belongs to and WHERE that group lives.
% Recall is a binding query:
%
%     ? foot_region(metatarsals, $Region)      % midfoot
%
% This is a native `table` (ADJ-TABLES RS-5): each `row` lowers to a ground
% relation `foot_region(group, region)` carrying the table's citation, so the
% lookup above is the ordinary SLD binding query — the engine returns the
% region WITH its source, on the CPU, with zero model calls, and abstains on a
% word that is not one of these three foot-bone groups (it never invents a
% region).
%
% Each of the three groups of foot bones and the region / part of the foot the
% source states for it, as a little truth table:
%
%     bone group    region / part of the foot   a beginner's cue
%     -----------   -------------------------   ------------------------------
%     tarsals       heel_and_ankle              the cluster at the back of the foot
%     metatarsals   midfoot                     the long bones of the body / sole
%     phalanges     toes                        the little bones of the toes
%
% The query also runs BACKWARD — bind a region and recall its bone group:
%
%     ? foot_region($Group, toes)   % phalanges
%
% A word that is NOT one of these three foot-bone groups — `carpals` (those are
% the WRIST bones, not the foot), `femur`, `rock` — is deliberately NOT a row,
% so a foot-bone recall ABSTAINS on it rather than inventing a region. That
% honest-abstention property is what makes the table safe to build a reasoner
% on: it answers exactly the group→region pairs it can ground, and only those.
%
% The `region` value of every row is a SINGLE lowercase atom, chosen to be a
% region / part of the foot the source EXPLICITLY states for that group —
% never one the source does not support. Where the source pairs two everyday
% words for one region (the tarsals "form the heel and the ankle") the atom
% joins them with an underscore (`heel_and_ankle`) so the value stays literally
% checkable against the quoted span; where the source names one word for the
% region (`midfoot`, `toes`) the atom is that word.
%
% ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
% Provenance (nothing here is asserted from memory — every group→region pair
% below was read out of a fetched Wikipedia foot-anatomy page, and any pair
% whose region could not be verified there would have been dropped). All three
% pairs are grounded by a SINGLE defining sentence of the "Metatarsal bones"
% article, which locates all three groups relative to one another:
%
%   tarsals → heel_and_ankle
%   metatarsals → midfoot
%   phalanges → toes
%     https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Metatarsal_bones
%     (Wikipedia — "Metatarsal bones")
%     "The metatarsal bones, collectively the metatarsus (pl.: metatarsi), are
%      a group of five long bones in the midfoot, located between the tarsal
%      bones (which form the heel and the ankle) and the phalanges (toes)."
%
% That one sentence fixes every row at once: the metatarsals are "in the
% midfoot"; the tarsal bones "form the heel and the ankle"; and the phalanges
% are "(toes)". An ADJ `table` carries ONE provenance envelope, so the
% `source`/`locator`/`trust` below hold that single sentence — the cleanest
% span, which grounds all three rows together. Wikipedia is a tertiary,
% community-edited encyclopedia rather than a primary government or university
% anatomy resource, so `trust consensus` (not `authoritative`).
% (See ../README.md; feedback_nothing_human_authored,
% feedback_adj_only_shipped_artifacts.)
% ============================================================================

table foot_region {
    columns group, region

    row (tarsals, heel_and_ankle)
    row (metatarsals, midfoot)
    row (phalanges, toes)

    source "The metatarsal bones, collectively the metatarsus (pl.: metatarsi), are a group of five long bones in the midfoot, located between the tarsal bones (which form the heel and the ankle) and the phalanges (toes)."
    locator "https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Metatarsal_bones"
    trust consensus
}
